How does a turtle find its way across an ocean, and back to the same coast years later? In laboratory tanks in the USA, scientists placed hatchlings inside coils that could change the magnetic field around them. The babies changed direction to match, as if they were reading a map.

The leading idea is that each stretch of coast has its own magnetic pattern, and a young turtle remembers the pattern of the beach where it hatched. This is strongly supported, but scientists are still studying it.

{{story:earth-magnet}}

There are only two kinds of alligator. The American alligator lives in the south-eastern United States, from the Carolinas to Texas. The Chinese alligator is small and very rare, surviving along the lower Yangtze River.

Crocodiles are far more widespread, across Africa, Asia, Australia and the Americas. South Florida is the one place where both live in the wild together. In the [[@florida-everglades|Everglades]], a lucky visitor can see a dark American alligator in a freshwater pond and a pale American crocodile in a salty bay on the same day.

{{story:birds-dinosaurs}}

White school glue is sold as PVA, and under brand names like Fevicol in India and Elmer's in the USA. It is tiny bits of sticky plastic floating in water. As the water soaks away and evaporates, the plastic bits crowd together and fuse into a clear, bendy film.

[[@accident-superglue|Superglue]] works differently. It reacts with the tiny amount of water that is almost always present in the air and on surfaces. That reaction links its molecules into long chains within seconds. Epoxy glue comes in two tubes. Mixed together, the two liquids react and harden into a tough [[@plastic-made|plastic]].

{{story:plastic-made}}

Advertising is old. Shop signs and painted notices were common in ancient Roman towns. After the [[@printing-press|printing press]] spread in Europe, printed notices and newspaper ads followed.

On 1 July 1941, a New York TV station showed what is usually called the first official television commercial in the USA. It was for Bulova watches: a clock over a map of America, lasting about ten seconds. In India, adverts appeared on [[@doordarshan-tech|Doordarshan]] in the 1970s. By the 1980s, [[@behind-theme-music|jingles]] for soaps, cold drinks and toothpastes were being hummed by children all over the country.

{{story:amul}}

In 1947, as British rule ended, the border between [[@pakistan-history|India and Pakistan]] had to be drawn fast. A British lawyer named Cyril Radcliffe, who had never been to India before, was given about five weeks.

He worked with old maps and [[census|census]] figures, deciding which villages went where. The line was published two days after independence. Millions of people found themselves on the side they did not want, and the [[@partition|Partition]] that followed was one of the largest movements of people in history.

{{story:partition}}

The idea is very old. The Roman emperor Augustus put a tax of one hundredth on goods sold at auction. Merchants in [[medieval|medieval]] Italy worked out interest on loans "per cento", for every hundred.

When writing by hand, they shortened "per cento" in different ways. Historians have traced how one shorthand, a "p" followed by a small looped mark, slowly changed over a couple of centuries into the sign % that we use now. [[@taxes|Taxes]] and trade have been driving maths for a long time.

{{story:taxes}}

A famous story says that when Carl Friedrich Gauss was a young boy in Germany, his teacher told the class to add up every number from 1 to 100. Gauss wrote the answer almost at once: 5,050. The story may have grown in the telling, but the trick is real.

Pair the numbers from each end: 1 + 100, 2 + 99, 3 + 98. Every pair makes 101, and there are 50 pairs. So the total is 50 × 101 = 5,050. That gives a formula for any triangular number: multiply n by n + 1, then halve it.

{{story:pyramids}}

Guinness first decides whether a category is allowed. It has to be measurable, breakable and possible to repeat under the same rules. Then the person attempting it must send evidence: video, photos, measurements and statements from independent witnesses. Sometimes an official judge, called an adjudicator, attends in person.

Some records are refused or retired for safety. Guinness will not accept record attempts that put people or animals in danger, such as the heaviest pet.

{{story:measuring-units}}

1780 के दशक में इटली के वैज्ञानिक लुइजी गैल्वानी ने देखा कि धातु के औज़ार छूने पर मरे हुए मेंढकों की टाँगें फड़क उठती थीं। उन्होंने कहा कि जानवरों के अंदर अपनी "[[@power-electric-eel|जानवरों वाली बिजली]]" होती है। उनके प्रयोगों ने [[@electricity-history|बिजली]] में ज़बरदस्त दिलचस्पी जगाई और पहली बैटरियों तक का रास्ता खोला।

गैल्वानी आंशिक रूप से सही थे। नस-संकेत बिजली वाले ही होते हैं, पर वे तार के करंट से अलग तरह से काम करते हैं। न्यूरॉन अपनी झिल्ली के आर-पार सोडियम और पोटैशियम जैसे [[charge|आवेश]] वाले कणों को अंदर-बाहर पंप करता है। एक संकेत इन्हीं कणों की एक लहर है, जो नन्हे-नन्हे दरवाज़ों से होकर, एक हिस्से के बाद दूसरे में, कोशिका की पूरी लंबाई में दौड़ जाती है।

{{story:giant-squid}}

In 1983 an American programmer, Richard Stallman, started a project called GNU to build a complete operating system that anyone could use and share freely. He believed people should be free to study and change the software on their own computers.

By 1991 GNU had many parts but still lacked its central piece, the kernel. That year Linus Torvalds, a 21-year-old student in Helsinki, posted online that he was writing one. It was "just a hobby", he wrote, and "won't be big and professional". Thousands of volunteers joined in. Combined with GNU tools, his Linux kernel became a complete free system.

{{story:libraries}}

NASA's twin rovers Spirit and Opportunity landed on Mars in 2004. Each was meant to work for about 90 Martian days. Spirit lasted six years. Opportunity kept going for more than 14 years and drove about 45 km, more than a marathon. A huge dust storm in 2018 finally covered its solar panels.

The newer rovers, Curiosity and Perseverance, are as big as a small car and powered by nuclear batteries. Perseverance collects rock samples in tubes for a future mission to bring home. Because radio signals take minutes to cross the gap, the rovers steer themselves around rocks using cameras and software.

{{story:mars-rovers}}

On 3 November 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 2 carrying Laika, a stray dog from the streets of Moscow. She became the first animal to orbit Earth. Scientists wanted to know if a living thing could survive launch and weightlessness.

There was no way to bring her back. For decades, Soviet officials said she lived several days. In 2002 a scientist revealed she had died within hours, as her capsule overheated. Three years later, in 1960, two dogs named Belka and Strelka orbited Earth and came home safely. Their flight helped pave the way for the first human in space in 1961, part of the story of [[@space-animals|animals in space]].

{{story:space-animals}}

Naples, one of Italy's biggest cities, lies just a few kilometres from Vesuvius. In 79 CE, Vesuvius buried the Roman towns of [[@pompeii|Pompeii]] and Herculaneum in ash. Today about 6 lakh people live in the "red zone" nearest the volcano, with detailed plans to evacuate if it stirs.

[[@mexico|Mexico]] City lives within sight of Popocatépetl, which often puffs ash. [[@seattle|Seattle]], in the US, has Mount Rainier on its skyline. In 1980, nearby Mount St Helens erupted, blowing away the top of the mountain. Auckland, [[@new-zealand|New Zealand]]'s largest city, is built on a field of about 50 small volcanoes.

{{story:pompeii}}

The Philippine tarsier fits in your palm. Its eyes are so large it can't move them, so it swivels its head round to look straight behind it, like an owl. The Philippine eagle, one of the biggest eagles alive, has a wingspan of about 2 m and hunts flying lemurs and monkeys.

Under water it gets even busier. The Philippines sits in the Coral Triangle, which holds more kinds of reef fish than anywhere else on the planet. Tubbataha Reef, in the middle of the Sulu Sea, is so far from land that rangers live there on a platform for weeks at a time. Off Donsol, whale sharks as long as a school bus cruise past swimmers, eating [[plankton|plankton]].

{{story:coral-reefs}}

Amrit was guarded by the gods in heaven. Garuda flew there, battled the guards, put out a ring of fire by carrying water in his beak, and slipped past a spinning wheel of blades. He seized the pot.

On the way back, Vishnu was so impressed that he offered Garuda a gift. Garuda agreed to become Vishnu's carrier, and Vishnu promised him immortality. Garuda gave the snakes the amrit and freed his mother. But before the snakes could drink, Indra snatched it back. The snakes licked the grass where it had rested, and the sharp grass split their tongues. That, the story says, is why snakes have forked tongues.

{{story:myths-indian-creatures}}

After the [[@world-war-two|Second World War]], Japan was short of food. A businessman named Momofuku Ando saw long queues for bowls of ramen in Osaka and wondered whether noodles could be made that anyone could cook at home in minutes.

He experimented for about a year in a small shed behind his house. The breakthrough came when he watched his wife frying tempura. Frying drove the water out of the batter and left it full of tiny holes. He fried seasoned noodles the same way, and in 1958 he sold the first packets of Chikin Ramen. In 1971 he followed it with Cup Noodles, sold in a cup you could eat from. Today, according to the World Instant Noodles Association, people eat more than 100 billion servings of instant noodles a year, with China, Indonesia and India among the biggest eaters.

